sec.p:
The selected SNPs were used as instrumental variables (IVs) in the associated two-sample MR study. We utilized the R24 statistic to determine the proportion of variance in testosterone and oestradiol levels accounted for by each SNP25, with the instrument strength of each SNP being evaluated via the F statistic26 (calculated as\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\:\:\text{F}=\frac{{R}^{2}}{1-{R}^{2}}\times\:\frac{N-K-1}{K}$$\end{document},\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\:{R}^{2}=2\times\:MAF\times\:(1-MAF)\times\:{\left(\frac{\beta\:}{sd}\right)}^{2}$$\end{document}). Palindromic SNPs and those with specific allele frequencies were not included in our research27. If there was no corresponding SNP in the GWAS generated for a particular request, SNPs (proxies) with LD relationships with the requested SNP (target) were searched.
